{
 "id": "2026-chen-rooftop",
 "name": "Rooftop Green Spaces: Activity and Social Well-being",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the original scenes, shown as flat images instead of in VR: 27 rendered views of one open rooftop garden in Chengdu, built from a real podium-roof garden and varied in three steps on each of three design variables, functional type (A1 recreation and sightseeing, A2 catering and entertainment, A3 sports and fitness), green view index (B1 32.8%, B2 19.8%, B3 10.5%) and sky view factor (C1 27.5%, C2 17.4%, C3 9.2%). Participants see all 27 scenes in random order, each for 20 s, with a short break halfway. After each scene they rate, on 7-point scales, how willing they would be to do six activities there during a work break (walking, exercise, eating and drinking, viewing, conversation, reading) and the five social-health senses of the paper's supplement (belonging, support, trust, pleasure, safety). Rebuilt from Chen, Shen, Zhu, Gan, Zhou, Fang & Zeng (2026), Assessing the restorative benefits of open rooftop green spaces characteristics using virtual reality and EEG analysis, Frontiers in Psychology 17: 1807425, with the 27 scenes taken from its Table 1 (CC BY 4.0). Image ids: A(function)B(GVI)C(SVF), e.g. A1B1C1 = recreation, high greenery, most sky. Analysis as in the paper: correlations and regressions of each rating on GVI, SVF and functional type (dummy coded, recreation / low GVI / low SVF as reference), plus ANOVA by functional type; the paper (97 students) found social senses and walking willingness highest with high GVI and high SVF, and exercise willingness highest in fitness rooftops with high GVI. Differences from the original study: (1) flat images of about 560 px on a screen instead of 360-degree panoramas in an HTC Vive headset; (2) no EEG, so the six EEG indices (attention, engagement, excitement, stress, relaxation, interest) are not measured, and there is no baseline recording, stress-induction task or eyes-closed rest; (3) the paper gives the six activities and the five social senses with their definitions but not the question wording or scale anchors, so the question stems and the 1-7 anchor labels are ours; (4) the paper's demographic and baseline stress questionnaire is not printed, so only age and gender are asked (our wording); (5) the break after 14 scenes is ours (the paper split the session into two blocks with a 5-minute rest without saying where); (6) the intro asking participants to imagine an office break on their building's rooftop is our wording, based on the paper's work-break framing.",
 "author": "Chen, Shen, Zhu, Gan, Zhou, Fang & Zeng",
 "year": "2026",
